summary.text:
The task is to define the primary symbolic meaning of "Fish" in Christian teaching and biblical interpretation, based on independent knowledge.
{
  "primary_symbolic_meaning": "The fish symbolizes Christ or Christianity, often through the Greek acronym ichthys (Iēsous Christos, Theou Yios, Sōtēr) representing Jesus as Savior, with additional ties to themes of abundance and discipleship.

content.text:
{
  "primary_meaning": "Jesus Christ (as Savior/Son of God, via the ΙΧΘΥΣ/Ichthys acrostic)",
  "primary_basis": [
    "Early Christian use of the Greek acrostic ΙΧΘΥΣ (Iēsous Christos Theou Yios Sōtēr) drawn from New Testament confessions of Jesus' identity (e.g., Matt 16:16; John 20:31; Rom 1:3-4)",
    "Matt 4:19 / Mark 1:17 (call of the disciples as fishers of men, linking fish imagery to Christ and his mission)",
    "John 21:1-14 (post-resurrection miraculous catch of fish associated with the risen Christ)",
    "Matt 14:13-21 / Mark 6:30-44 / Luke 9:10-17 / John 6:1-14 (feeding miracles involving fish)"
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "meaning": "Believers / Christians / the Church (as the 'catch' of the gospel)",
      "basis": [
        "Matt 4:19 / Mark 1:17 / Luke 5:1-11 (fishers of men; disciples as fishers, people as fish)",
        "John 21:1-14 (large catch of fish as image of mission and the gathered community)"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Divine provision, abundance, or eucharistic nourishment",
      "basis": [
        "Matt 14:13-21 and parallels (multiplication of loaves and fish)",
        "John 6 (Bread of Life discourse following the feeding; fish as part of the meal imagery)"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Typological sign of Christ's death, burial, and resurrection (via Jonah)",
      "basis": [
        "Jonah 1–2 (great fish)",
        "Matt 12:39-41 / Matt 16:4 / Luke 11:29-32 (Jesus cites the sign of Jonah)"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Baptism / conversion / new life in the waters",
      "basis": [
        "Association of fish with water and the fishers-of-men motif (Matt 4:19 etc.)",
        "Early Christian artistic and catechetical links between fish imagery and baptismal rebirth"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    }
  ],
  "has_settled_consensus": true,
  "confidence": "high",
  "notes": "The dominant, widely recognized Christian symbol of the fish is Christ himself via the Ichthys acrostic and early catacomb/art usage; related but secondary meanings (believers, provision, Jonah typology) are also standard in biblical interpretation and preaching. No major competing primary meaning displaces the Christ/Ichthys reading in mainstream Christian teaching."
}
